gm-method/rendering (a profiler showing three-mesh-bvh setBuffer as the #1 raycast self-cost): the root cost is _BufferStack.setBuffer allocating THREE fresh TypedArray views (new Float32Array/Uint16Array/Uint32Array(buffer)) on EVERY raycastFirst, called once per geometry per intersectObjects -- so a camera that raycasts N env BVH meshes every frame x M sites pays 3*N*M throwaway allocations/frame (witnessed N=94 env meshes live). THE FIX is a per-ArrayBuffer view cache: memoize {float32Array,uint16Array,uint32Array} in a WeakMap keyed by the buffer inside setBuffer; the BVH root buffer is built once at MeshBVH construction and immutable on raycast, so views are safe to reuse and GC-reclaim with the BVH. KEEP the stack push/pop re-entrancy (shapecast nested in raycast) untouched -- only memoize the allocation. SECOND win at the call site: hoist the per-raycast envMeshes.filter(m=>m.geometry?.boundsTree) into a single array refreshed only on env-set mutation (not per frame), and add a cheap ray-vs-world-bounding-sphere broad-phase to skip meshes the ray misses before they pay any BVH setup (computeBoundingSphere alongside computeBoundsTree so the sphere exists; fallback include-if-no-sphere is correctness-safe). WITNESS by the browser `profile` prefix over a heavy raycast loop (e.g. 120k accelerated raycasts): setBuffer/clearBuffer should DISAPPEAR from the worst-20 culprits (were #1), leaving only intersectRay/_raycastFirst/intersectTriangle (the irreducible traversal). Prove correctness separately: many raycasts against the same boundsTree must be byte-identical + hit the known distance (cached views must not corrupt results).